ll be focusable if it somehow gets focus? It does not have tabindex. So Tab from input will go to next focusable element after the ul (if any). The ul is not focusable, so we need to check: The order is input -> ul -> status -> maybe other elements after container? Actually status is inside container as well, after ul. So after input, the next sibling is ul (not focusable), then status (not focusable unless it has tabindex? It has class visually-hidden but no tabindex). So after container, the next element in DOM is the #dietary input (since container is before #dietary? Let's examine the original index.html: The form has inputs: attendee, city, dietary, submit. The Awesomplete container wraps the city input. So the DOM order: <label for="attendee">, <input id="attendee">, <label for="city">, <div class="awesomplete"> (container) containing <input id="city">, <ul>, <span>, </div>, <label for="dietary">, <input id="dietary">, <button>, <p>. So after the container div, the next element is the label for dietary, then the input. So Tab from city input will go to the next focusable element after the container: that is the label? Labels are not focusable unless they have tabindex. Then the dietary input is focusable. So indeed removing tabindex from li should cause Tab to skip over the ul and its children and go to dietary input.

